// What stone becomes when a structure fails (`stone.ron`'s `breaks_into`).
//
// Distinct from `gravel` on purpose, and the difference is the point rather
// than decoration: gravel is a *loose aggregate* a player paints, while this
// is *broken rock* — the same material that was holding a ceiling up a
// moment ago, so it keeps stone's own greys rather than gravel's browner
// river-worn tones. Seeing a collapsed span land in the colour it fell in is
// what makes a collapse read as breakage instead of as a material swap.
//
// The colour is the load-bearing difference; the angles are close to
// cosmetic, and that is worth stating plainly because it is easy to assume
// otherwise. Measured against gravel in `examples/ascii.rs`'s repose scene,
// a rubble pile comes out at rows 1/5/7/11/15/17 against gravel's
// 1/5/8/11/14/17 — very slightly steeper, not visibly blockier. The reason
// is that reach is quantised: past 45 degrees `roll_reach_base` drops below
// 1 and the fractional part is spent as a per-grain *chance* (see
// `Material::reach_from_base`), so at 55 degrees a grain still steps
// sideways about 70% of the time. Reaching a genuinely blocky heap this way
// would need an angle no crushed rock actually has (~40-45 degrees in
// reality), which is not a trade worth making for a difference the colour
// already carries.
//
// So: do not expect this material to be what makes a collapse read as
// *chunks*. That is a coherent multi-cell body's job, and this is the
// fallback for failures too small or too large to be worth promoting to one.
(
    name: "rubble",
    display: "Rubble",
    kind: Powder,
    density: 2.0,
    friction_angle: 55.0,
    max_stability_angle: 65.0,
    // See Material::blast_resistance -- already-broken rock, a loose pile
    // like gravel rather than the intact stone it fell from.
    blast_resistance: 0.4,
    colors: [
        (124, 124, 128),
        (106, 106, 112),
        (140, 138, 142),
        (92, 94, 100),
        (132, 130, 134),
    ],
)
